{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And Joshua heard, etc., in its breaking, etc.</b> One needs to know what the verse intends by saying <b>in its breaking (בְּרֵעֹה)</b>, and what this voice that he heard was. Also, the response of Moses requires a prophet to tell the meaning of the words." "And the verses may be explained according to the statement of the Sages, of blessed memory (Yerushalmi Ta'anit 4:5), that the righteous discern in a voice whether it is for righteousness or for its overturning. And this perception is a divine perception that HaShem bequeathed to the great ones of the world. And this is what Joshua perceived, of which the verse says <b>and Joshua heard the voice of the people</b> — meaning, the articulation of a voice that was heard from the camp of Israel; he heard in it an aspect of evil. And this is what he meant by <b>in its breaking (בְּרֵעֹה)</b>. <b>And he said to Moses, the voice of battle in the camp</b> — he said to him that this voice he was hearing was a voice of battle, and this perception is the aspect of evil that he recognized in the voice. Or it may be that by saying <b>voice of battle</b> he alluded to the battle of the adversary, who is Samael, who is always present in the array of battle against man. And Moses responded, <b>There is no voice of answer of strength</b> — meaning, they were not overpowered by their evil inclination; <b>and there is no voice of answer of weakness</b> — meaning, not an aspect of weakness alone; <b>but a voice of answer</b> — for the staff of might and glory was broken, and the intention is that their deeds did not turn evil in one particular matter of the Torah's particulars that would weaken a man but not kill him; rather, they uprooted everything, since idolatry is weighed against the entire Torah (Ḥullin 5a)." "Or perhaps by placing the word <b>Anochi</b> adjacent to <b>anot</b> he alluded to the Shekhinah, in the sod of (Deuteronomy 32:18) ״צוּר יְלָדְךָ תֶּשִׁי״. And this is how it is to be read: ״the voice of the anot of Anochi, I hear״, and the word <b>I hear</b> refers back to Joshua, who said ״the voice of battle <b>in the camp</b>״ — meaning to say that this voice he was hearing was a voice that would declare the breaking of the Shekhinah, in the sod of (Proverbs 10:1) ״וּבֵן כְּסִיל תּוּגַת אִמּוֹ״. After I wrote this, I found a statement of the Zohar (Zohar vol. 2, 195a), and this is its language: What is ״in its breaking (בְּרֵעֹה)״ etc.? That voice was from the side of the Other Side, and Joshua looked at that voice which was from the side of evil; immediately ״and he said to Moses״ — end quote. And it accords with all that the Or HaChaim has written."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:36:08.556961611Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}